[127.448,"h",{"answer":"# The Keeper\n\nThomas had tended the lighthouse for thirty-seven years. He climbed the iron stairs each dawn without thinking, his weathered hands moving through the ritual of oil and glass by memory alone. The beam swept the darkness every night—a constant promise to the ships below.\n\nMost people didn't understand the work. They imagined solitude as something broken by storm and rescue, dramatic gestures lighting lost vessels to safety. The truth was quieter. Thomas spent his days maintaining small things: polishing glass that never quite stayed clean, oiling mechanisms that needed constant attention, recording the weather in ledgers no one read anymore. The beam did its work without fanfare, and the ships passed safely by.\n\nHis daughter called once a month. She wanted him to retire, move closer to the grandchildren, enjoy","durationMs":4177,"isAborted":true,"turnId":"158679e1-c1ca-4534-816b-1f0904e9af78","reason":"aborted","usage":{"input_tokens":10,"output_tokens":4,"cache_read_input_tokens":29313,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10931,"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001"},"hook_event_name":"turn.complete"}]
